WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.674
A California city is ready to build a library wing, a fire station, or a parking structure.

2
00:00:04.674 --> 00:00:08.887
The plans are drawn, but borrowing costs have risen since the architect was hired.

3
00:00:08.887 --> 00:00:12.020
The civic question is not simply whether the project is good.

4
00:00:12.020 --> 00:00:18.749
It is who controls the economic tools, how those tools reach a local budget, and what tradeoff residents are being asked to accept.

5
00:00:18.749 --> 00:00:20.259
Study preparation only.

6
00:00:20.259 --> 00:00:25.841
This episode does not award credit or determine whether any school will grant credit.

7
00:00:25.841 --> 00:00:26.772
Start with money.

8
00:00:26.772 --> 00:00:31.043
Money serves as a medium of exchange, a unit of account, and a store of value.

9
00:00:31.043 --> 00:00:34.000
Currency is money, but so are checkable bank deposits.

10
00:00:34.000 --> 00:00:35.972
Banks connect savers with borrowers.

11
00:00:35.972 --> 00:00:38.819
They accept deposits, keep reserves, and make loans.

12
00:00:38.819 --> 00:00:42.324
When banks lend, deposits can expand through the banking system.

13
00:00:42.324 --> 00:00:45.007
That does not mean banks create unlimited wealth.

14
00:00:45.007 --> 00:00:50.155
Lending is constrained by regulation, capital, reserves, risk, and the demand for sound loans.

15
00:00:50.155 --> 00:00:54.591
Interest is the price paid to borrow funds and the return earned by lending them.

16
00:00:54.591 --> 00:00:58.314
A higher rate discourages some borrowing and encourages some saving.

17
00:00:58.314 --> 00:01:05.543
For a city, the relevant rate may be the yield investors demand on municipal bonds, not the rate shown in a household advertisement.

18
00:01:05.543 --> 00:01:08.500
Still, rates move through connected financial markets.

19
00:01:08.500 --> 00:01:15.071
A project that looked affordable at one rate can become difficult at another even if its construction price stays fixed.

20
00:01:15.071 --> 00:01:23.450
Monetary policy is the Federal Reserve's management of money and credit conditions in pursuit of goals that include maximum employment and stable prices.

21
00:01:23.450 --> 00:01:29.364
The Fed can adjust administered rates, conduct open market operations, and influence the federal funds rate.

22
00:01:29.364 --> 00:01:32.759
A lower policy rate tends to encourage borrowing and spending.

23
00:01:32.759 --> 00:01:35.661
A higher rate tends to restrain demand and inflation.

24
00:01:35.661 --> 00:01:36.921
The effect is indirect.

25
00:01:36.921 --> 00:01:40.918
The Fed does not order a family to buy a car or a council to issue bonds.

26
00:01:40.918 --> 00:01:42.452
Policy also works with lags.

27
00:01:42.452 --> 00:01:46.395
Banks, firms, households, and governments need time to change decisions.

28
00:01:46.395 --> 00:01:50.064
An interest rate increase today may slow construction months later.

29
00:01:50.064 --> 00:01:54.883
An interest rate cut may not produce much new borrowing if businesses expect weak sales.

30
00:01:54.883 --> 00:01:57.949
This is why monetary policy is powerful but not precise.

31
00:01:57.949 --> 00:02:00.687
Fiscal policy is taxation and government spending.

32
00:02:00.687 --> 00:02:04.466
At the federal level, Congress and the president make fiscal choices.

33
00:02:04.466 --> 00:02:07.970
California's Legislature and governor make state fiscal choices.

34
00:02:07.970 --> 00:02:12.297
A city council adopts a local budget within state law and local revenue limits.

35
00:02:12.297 --> 00:02:15.637
These governments interact, but they are not interchangeable.

36
00:02:15.637 --> 00:02:17.937
A federal tax cut is not a city ordinance.

37
00:02:17.937 --> 00:02:20.401
A state grant is not permanent local revenue.

38
00:02:20.401 --> 00:02:27.137
During a recession, expansionary fiscal policy can raise spending, reduce taxes, or allow automatic stabilizers to operate.

39
00:02:27.137 --> 00:02:32.887
Programs such as unemployment insurance rise when need rises, while tax collections fall as incomes fall.

40
00:02:32.887 --> 00:02:36.665
These changes support demand without requiring a new vote each month.

41
00:02:36.665 --> 00:02:42.908
During excessive inflation, contractionary fiscal policy can reduce demand through lower spending or higher taxes.

42
00:02:42.908 --> 00:02:47.617
In practice, elected officials face recognition, legislative, and implementation lags.

43
00:02:47.617 --> 00:02:50.465
A response can arrive after conditions have shifted.

44
00:02:50.465 --> 00:02:52.546
Budgets also create deficits and debt.

45
00:02:52.546 --> 00:02:56.105
A deficit is the gap in one period when spending exceeds revenue.

46
00:02:56.105 --> 00:02:58.296
Debt is accumulated borrowing over time.

47
00:02:58.296 --> 00:03:02.951
Borrowing can spread the cost of a long-lived asset across residents who will use it.

48
00:03:02.951 --> 00:03:05.962
It can also require future taxes and interest payments.

49
00:03:05.962 --> 00:03:12.479
Ask what was financed, who benefits, what repayment is promised, and what other spending the debt service may displace.

50
00:03:12.479 --> 00:03:17.251
Economic growth means an increase in an economy's capacity to produce goods and services.

51
00:03:17.251 --> 00:03:21.754
Over long periods, growth in real output per person depends heavily on productivity.

52
00:03:21.754 --> 00:03:28.992
Workers produce more when they have better education, useful technology, sound infrastructure, and capital such as tools and equipment.

53
00:03:28.992 --> 00:03:30.333
Institutions also matter.

54
00:03:30.333 --> 00:03:35.051
Reliable law, functioning finance, and incentives for innovation can support investment.

55
00:03:35.051 --> 00:03:38.321
Growth is not the same as a temporary rise in total spending.

56
00:03:38.321 --> 00:03:42.879
Fiscal or monetary stimulus may help an economy use idle resources during a downturn.

57
00:03:42.879 --> 00:03:46.846
Long-run growth requires more resources or more output from each resource.

58
00:03:46.846 --> 00:03:53.280
A city contributes through basics such as safe streets, water systems, permitting, transportation, and workforce access.

59
00:03:53.280 --> 00:03:55.853
Yet every local investment has opportunity cost.

60
00:03:55.853 --> 00:03:59.928
Money used for a convention center cannot also replace a failing sewer line.

61
00:03:59.928 --> 00:04:05.343
Trade can expand consumption because people and places specialize according to comparative advantage.

62
00:04:05.343 --> 00:04:10.329
Comparative advantage concerns lower opportunity cost, not simply being the fastest producer.

63
00:04:10.329 --> 00:04:14.029
When trading partners specialize and exchange, total output can rise.

64
00:04:14.029 --> 00:04:18.050
Gains from trade do not guarantee that every worker, firm, or region gains.

65
00:04:18.050 --> 00:04:23.840
Imports can lower prices for consumers while creating concentrated adjustment costs for competing producers.

66
00:04:23.840 --> 00:04:27.111
Exchange rates tell how much one currency trades for another.

67
00:04:27.111 --> 00:04:30.488
If the dollar appreciates, a dollar buys more foreign currency.

68
00:04:30.488 --> 00:04:37.619
Imported equipment may become cheaper for a California city, while United States exports may become more expensive to foreign buyers.

69
00:04:37.619 --> 00:04:40.622
If the dollar depreciates, the pattern tends to reverse.

70
00:04:40.622 --> 00:04:47.484
Exchange rates respond to supply and demand for currencies, including trade, investment flows, interest rates, and expectations.

71
00:04:47.484 --> 00:04:50.111
The trade balance and financial flows are linked.

72
00:04:50.111 --> 00:04:57.242
A country that imports more goods and services than it exports must have an offsetting net inflow in the broader balance of payments.

73
00:04:57.242 --> 00:05:02.335
That accounting link does not by itself tell you whether a particular trade deficit is harmful.

74
00:05:02.335 --> 00:05:08.716
The effect depends partly on whether foreign funds support productive investment, consumption, or government borrowing.

75
00:05:08.716 --> 00:05:11.611
Now bring the concepts to a California council agenda.

76
00:05:11.611 --> 00:05:14.667
Suppose a library expansion costs twenty million dollars.

77
00:05:14.667 --> 00:05:19.331
The city expects a five million dollar state grant and plans to borrow fifteen million.

78
00:05:19.331 --> 00:05:22.387
Staff first estimated debt service when rates were lower.

79
00:05:22.387 --> 00:05:28.285
The council should request annual payments under the old rate, the current rate, and a reasonable stress case.

80
00:05:28.285 --> 00:05:30.805
The difference is not abstract monetary policy.

81
00:05:30.805 --> 00:05:34.397
It is fewer dollars available for staff, books, parks, or reserves.

82
00:05:34.397 --> 00:05:38.204
The grant changes construction financing but may not pay ongoing costs.

83
00:05:38.204 --> 00:05:43.833
The new wing needs employees, electricity, insurance, maintenance, and eventual replacement of equipment.

84
00:05:43.833 --> 00:05:47.425
Separate one-time capital sources from recurring operating sources.

85
00:05:47.425 --> 00:05:48.980
A ribbon-cutting occurs once.

86
00:05:48.980 --> 00:05:50.750
Payroll arrives every pay period.

87
00:05:50.750 --> 00:05:53.537
Local revenue may also move with the larger economy.

88
00:05:53.537 --> 00:05:55.950
Sales tax receipts can weaken in a recession.

89
00:05:55.950 --> 00:05:57.773
Hotel taxes can respond to travel.

90
00:05:57.773 --> 00:06:02.974
Property tax is often steadier but operates under California's legal rules and assessment system.

91
00:06:02.974 --> 00:06:05.869
At the same time, demand for public help may increase.

92
00:06:05.869 --> 00:06:14.179
A city usually cannot run an operating deficit with the flexibility of the federal government, so national expansion and local cutbacks can occur together.

93
00:06:14.179 --> 00:06:16.485
Trade and exchange rates can alter the bid.

94
00:06:16.485 --> 00:06:20.881
Imported electrical equipment may become more expensive after dollar depreciation.

95
00:06:20.881 --> 00:06:23.830
Tariffs or supply disruptions can raise material costs.

96
00:06:23.830 --> 00:06:31.014
Staff should distinguish a general price change from a contractor's individual markup and should document the source of each estimate.

97
00:06:31.014 --> 00:06:42.702
The decision memo should therefore show at least five items: confirmed grant funds, the amount borrowed, annual debt service under multiple rates, recurring operating cost, and the revenue source exposed to a downturn.

98
00:06:42.702 --> 00:06:44.686
It should also name the alternatives.

99
00:06:44.686 --> 00:06:49.672
The council might borrow now, phase construction, reduce scope, seek another grant, or delay.

100
00:06:49.672 --> 00:06:51.012
Each choice carries risk.

101
00:06:51.012 --> 00:06:56.159
Delay may avoid expensive borrowing, but construction prices may rise and residents wait longer.

102
00:06:56.159 --> 00:06:59.752
Borrowing now may lock in certainty, but it may crowd out services.

103
00:06:59.752 --> 00:07:02.164
Good civic analysis keeps authority straight.

104
00:07:02.164 --> 00:07:05.220
The council cannot vote to change the federal funds rate.

105
00:07:05.220 --> 00:07:11.118
It can choose project timing, debt structure within legal limits, reserves, local revenues, and project scope.

106
00:07:11.118 --> 00:07:15.836
It can advocate to state and federal officials, but advocacy is not the same as control.

107
00:07:15.836 --> 00:07:17.114
Three recall questions.

108
00:07:17.114 --> 00:07:20.170
First, how do monetary policy and fiscal policy differ?

109
00:07:20.170 --> 00:07:23.337
Second, why does productivity matter for long-run growth?

110
00:07:23.337 --> 00:07:28.560
Third, how can an appreciating dollar affect a city's imported equipment and a local exporter?

111
00:07:28.560 --> 00:07:34.581
First answer: monetary policy influences money, credit, and interest conditions through the Federal Reserve.

112
00:07:34.581 --> 00:07:40.824
Fiscal policy uses government taxing and spending decisions made by elected branches at their respective levels.

113
00:07:40.824 --> 00:07:49.353
Second answer: productivity allows workers to produce more output from available labor and resources, supporting higher real output per person over time.

114
00:07:49.353 --> 00:07:58.384
Third answer: an appreciating dollar can make imported equipment cheaper in dollar terms while making the local exporter's goods more expensive to foreign buyers.

115
00:07:58.384 --> 00:08:03.393
Artifact: write a one-page briefing for a California city capital project.

116
00:08:03.393 --> 00:08:18.895
Include current and stress-case debt service, confirmed grant dollars, recurring year-five operating costs, one recession-sensitive revenue source, one trade or exchange-rate exposure, and two alternatives to the proposed timing.

117
00:08:18.895 --> 00:08:21.806
Mark every unsupported estimate as unknown.

118
00:08:21.806 --> 00:08:52.336
Source note: This original study script draws on OpenStax, Principles of Macroeconomics 3e, especially Chapter 7, Economic Growth; Chapter 14, Money and Banking; Chapter 15, Monetary Policy and Bank Regulation; Chapter 16, Exchange Rates and International Capital Flows; Chapter 17, Government Budgets and Fiscal Policy; Chapter 18, The Impacts of Government Borrowing; Chapter 20, International Trade; and Chapter 21, Globalization and Protectionism.

